  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Orcs are a fantasy race dating back to Tolkien’s works, and since then they\n\thave served as a generic, barbaric, evil humanoid in countless works of\n\tfiction. However, official DnD lore also inludes occasional examples of\n\tplaying against type, including the\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Many-Arrows Clan",
              "href": "https://forgottenrealms.fandom.com/wiki/Many-Arrows"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\tin the Forgotten Realms. Whether you want to play to the trope or play against\n\ttype, the Orc is an iconic fantasy race worth playing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the original version of the Orc is a solid melee monster with a\n\tfew unique traits. The Orc’s ability score increases, coupled with Aggressive,\n\tmake it easy to get into melee and move between targets. Darkvision and some\n\textra skills are great, too, frequently giving martial classes like the\n\tBarbarian and the Fighter something to do outside of combat. Unfortunately,\n\tthe custom origin rules essentially made the Orc a worse tabaxi."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Orc published in Monsters of the Multiverse is all\n\tabout durability. Between Relentless Endurance and Adrenaline Rush, it’s very\n\thard to bring an orc down with hit point damage. Of course, the ability to\n\twithstand a lot of damage doesn’t help you actively accomplish many things, so\n\tthe Orc is once again pigeon-holed into a front-line martial role where their\n\ttraits are consistently put to use. Borrowing Relentless Endurance from the\n\tHalf-Orc also means that Adrenaline Rush needs to be better than Savage\n\tCritical and a free skill proficiency, which is a hard trade."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-versions",
      "heading": "Orc Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There are effectively 5 versions of the Orc. While that gives them as many\n\tversions as the Aasimar and the Eladrin, the Orc’s version history is less\n\tcomplex because it flows in a single line rather than having parallel\n\tversions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Version one of the Orc was published in Volo’s Guide to Monsters. It, along\n\twith the Kobold, were the only races in 5th edition to ever include an ability\n\tscore decrease as part of their traits. This decrease was later removed in\n\terrata. We’ll call the errata version 1.1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Orc later appeared in both Eberron: Rising From the Last War and in\n\tExplorer’s Guide to Wildemount. This version replaced the Menacing trait with\n\tPrimal Intuition, which allowed players to select two skills from a fixed list\n\trather than only getting Intimidation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The release of Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything gave us the custom origin\n\trules, giving us the next version of the Orc. This added some flexibility to\n\tthe Orc, but their signature trait, Aggressive, still pigeon-holes them into\n\tmelee roles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-versions-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Monsters of the Multiverse fully rewrote the Orc. Their skill\n\tproficiencies were removed entirely, Aggressive was replaced with the much\n\tmore useful Adrenaline Rush, and the Orc gained the Half-Orcs Relentless\n\tEndurance."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Orc Classes",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush will help compensate for the Artificer’s d8 hit dice, but\n\tmost artificers use their Bonus Action heavily so it’s hard to keep it\n\tavailable for Adrenaline Rush. Relentless Endurance is useful if you’re your\n\tparty’s primary Defender."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush may be useful late into combat if you need to switch targets,\n\tbut in most combats they’ll be within range of your 40 ft. speed. Many\n\tbarbarian subclasses also have a consistent use for your Bonus Action, so\n\tusing Adrenaline Rush is often difficult even in situations where it might be\n\tuseful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush provides both a helpful source of temporary hit points and a way to put extra distance between you and your enemies. Relentless Endurance might save your life from time to time. Neither will make bards good in melee, but extra durability is always nice."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush is a great way to rush to an allies aid or to get out of a\n\tdangerous spot. You don’t need to keep it available every round, but it’s good\n\twhen you desperately need to get into or out of melee, and you can sacrifice\n\tone round of Spiritual Weapon attacks. Relentless Endurance can keep your\n\tparty’s primary healer conscious."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush is a great way to rush to an allies aid or to get out of a dangerous spot, and Relentless Endurance can keep your party’s primary healer conscious. I think both Adrenaline Rush and Relentless Endurance work while in Wild Shape, too, so Circle of the Moon is a great combination."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Between Adrenaline Rush, Relentless Endurance, Second Wind, and decent armor,\n\tit’s very difficult to bring down an orc fighter. Grab a two-handed weapon and\n\tgo looking for trouble."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush is somewhat redundant with Step of the Wind, but Adrenaline\n\tRush both gives you temporary hit points and doesn’t consume Ki, so there is\n\tsome benefit there. Relentless Endurance is good insurance, especially at low\n\tlevels before your AC scales with your Ability Score Increases."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush is a great way for the Paladin to start combat. While\n\tpaladins have more uses for their Bonus Action than fighters, you still don’t\n\talways need them on turn 1 of combat. Relentless Endurance adds to the\n\tPaladin’s already impressive durability, giving you a good opportunity to dump\n\tall of your Lay on Hands point into healing yourself in a hurry."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rangers lean heavily on their Bonus Action, so using Adrenaline Rush presents\n\ta significant reduction to your damage output which is often hard to\n\tjustify."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush is partially redundant with Cunning Action, and using\n\tRelentless Endurance should be an extreme rarity for the Rogue. Between the\n\tease of getting out of melee and Uncanny Dodge, rogues are already plenty\n\tdurable."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush and Relentless Endurance provide some helpful insurance on a frail class, but they won’t make you better as a sorcerer. If you’re worried about being out of range, use a long-range spell or make enemies come to you."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush and Relentless Endurance provide some helpful insurance on a\n\tfrail class, but they won’t make you better as a warlock. If you’re worried\n\tabout being out of range, use a long-range spell or make enemies come to\n\tyou."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Adrenaline Rush and Relentless Endurance provide some helpful insurance on a\n\tfrail class, but they won’t make you better as a wizard. If you’re worried\n\tabout being out of range, use a long-range spell or make enemies come to\n\tyou."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Classes (Customizable Origins)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Aggressive is passable for melee armorer builds, but other artificers will\n\tfrequently use their Bonus Action every turn."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rage also uses your Bonus Action, and many barbarian subclasses have a Bonus\n\tAction option which you’ll use repeatedly, so it’s hard to use Aggressive on\n\tturn 1 when it really matters, and most barbarians won’t want to use it once\n\tthey’re raging."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you are far enough away that Aggressive seems like a good idea, you should\n\tbe casting spells."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Use Aggressive to charge into melee before activating Spirit Guardians, then\n\tuse Aggressive to chase your enemies when they try to escape your magical\n\tradiant damage blender. Probably best for cleric domains with heavy armor."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Many druid subclasses don’t use their Bonus Action, but druids also typically\n\tdon’t want to be in melee. Circle of the Moon is your best bet, but only\n\tbecause Aggressive should work while using Wild Shape. Just remember that\n\tusing Wild Shape will also use your Bonus Action, so you can’t do both in the\n\tsame turn."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fighters have few uses for their Bonus Action, so Aggressive works great for\n\tmelee builds. Two skills also help you do something useful outside of\n\tcombat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Aggressive is redundant with Step of the Wind, and monks get a scaling speed\n\tbonus that should make Aggressive unnecessary in almost any fight beyond low\n\tlevels."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Paladins are almost exclusively build for melee, so Aggressive works great as\n\tlong as you’re not using your Bonus Action for spells or Channel Divinity. Two\n\tskills will also help you expand beyond Face skills."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Ranger already relies heavily on their Bonus Action, so Aggressive is\n\thard to use, especially early in combat where Aggressive is most useful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Agressive is redundant with Cunning Action. Play a tabaxi if you desperately\n\twant more movement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you’re out of range to cast every one of your spells, your enemies are\n\talmost certainly out of range to hurt you, too. Use that time to give yourself\n\tand advantage rather than running screaming into their attack range with\n\tAggressive. The extra skills are great, but two skills and Darkvision is\n\tavailable from numerous races."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you’re out of range to cast every one of your spells, your enemies are\n\talmost certainly out of range to hurt you, too. Use that time to give yourself\n\tand advantage rather than running screaming into their attack range with\n\tAggressive. The extra skills are great, but two skills and Darkvision is\n\tavailable from numerous races."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you’re out of range to cast every one of your spells, your enemies are\n\talmost certainly out of range to hurt you, too. Use that time to give yourself\n\tand advantage rather than running screaming into their attack range with\n\tAggressive. The extra skills are great, but two skills and Darkvision is\n\tavailable from numerous races."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Orc is tailor-made to be a barbarian. Strength, Constitution, Aggressive,\n\tand Intimidate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Bard is likely the Orc’s nest option or arcane spellcasting, but I would\n\tstick to Valor so that you don’t need to invest heavily in Dexterity. Even\n\tthen, it’s not a great choice."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Cleric is the Orc’s best option for divine spellcasting, and if you stick\n\tto domains which provide proficiency in heavy armor you should be okay."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You might be able to use Aggressive during Wild Shape, but that’s not nearly\n\tenough to make the Orc a decent druid."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For a Strength-based build, it’s hard to go wrong with the Orc. Strength,\n\tConstitution, and Aggressive make it easy to get into melee and move between\n\ttargets, and the extra skill proficiency is always nice."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength-based monks don’t work."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The paladin isn’t as straightforward as the Barbarian or the Fighter, but the\n\tOrc still makes a fine paladin. Proficiency in Intimidation gives you a free\n\tface skill, so if you can manage decent Charisma the Paladin can be really\n\tgreat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Strength-based ranger build is difficult, but doable."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Dexterity increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard-2",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-2-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
